{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Domain requires this callback even for a storage-only consumer. The\n delegation test must use only node_path; discovering edges would be\n an unwanted graph access, which this guard rejects. Traversal fixtures\n separately exercise edge discovery."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is appropriate.\n\n- **Placement and form:** It sits on an explicit function with a body, as a fenced `:::{cite}` with commentary. The built-in allows this form.\n- **Reason is substantive:** The function is a deliberate `panic!` guard. The `Domain` trait requires the callback, but storage-only tests must never discover edges, so the guard is never executed. That is a real reason for zero execution, not just difficulty.\n- **Agrees with the source:** `StoredGraph`'s `node_path`, `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` delegate to `FileEvidenceStorage` using only `node_path`. The delegation test `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` calls only those methods. The `panic!` message matches the \"unwanted graph access\" claim.\n- **Other fixtures exist:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/validation.rs` (`impl[Memory/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]`) and `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs` implement and exercise edge discovery. This supports the claim that traversal fixtures cover it elsewhere.\n- **No redundancy:** The item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target.",
